---
# Slice 2 of the ingress default-deny. The baseline admits traefik, prometheus and the node bridges
# to every pod carrying `netpol-baseline: enforced`. This file adds radarr's own callers, which are
# all direct pod → ClusterIP and therefore invisible to the baseline.
#
# This rule is PORT-SCOPED, and for the callers below that scoping is the whole grant: the baseline
# admits none of janitorr, configarr, monitor-bridge, autofix-bridge, bazarr, prowlarr or homepage, so
# reach radarr only through this rule and only on this one port.
#
# TRAEFIK IS LISTED HERE DELIBERATELY — do not remove it as redundant — but be accurate about why.
# The baseline admits traefik to every baseline-labelled pod on ANY port, and policies are
# additive, so while radarr carries that label the union is the same with or without this entry.
# It was load-bearing for the deploy that INTRODUCED the label: the old pod carried `app: radarr`
# without `netpol-baseline: enforced`, so this policy was the only one selecting it and the UI
# would have 502'd for the length of that rollout. That window is closed — every pod created from
# here on carries both labels. It stays because this policy then states its own complete caller set
# rather than depending on the baseline continuing to admit traefik on all ports: if that baseline
# rule is ever port-scoped, or this pod template loses the label, this line is what keeps the UI up.
#
# bazarr addresses radarr by BARE NAME from its own application database, not from any manifest.
#
# prowlarr is here for the REVERSE of the radarr→prowlarr direction documented elsewhere: its
# Application Sync POSTs indexer definitions INTO radarr over the ClusterIP
# (`[POST] http://radarr:7878/api/v3/indexer?forceSave=true`, seen live in prowlarr's logs).
# Same class as bazarr above — configured in prowlarr's own application database on its PVC, not
# in any manifest here — so it is invisible to grep and easy to mistake for an unused caller.
# Fenced, radarr silently keeps stale indexer definitions and nothing alerts, because
# monitor-bridge's "Prowlarr Indexers" check reads prowlarr's own /api/v1/indexerstatus, which
# this breakage does not touch.
#
# homepage's dashboard widget reads /api/v3/queue over the ClusterIP
# (roles/k8s/homepage/templates/services.yaml.j2). It goes direct rather than through Traefik
# because radarr's public route is Authelia-gated and its monitoring IngressRoute exposes only
# that one path — so this entry is the whole grant, and fencing it out breaks the widget.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: radarr
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: radarr
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: traefik
        - podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values:
                  - janitorr
                  - configarr
                  - monitor-bridge
                  - autofix-bridge
                  - bazarr
                  - prowlarr        # Application Sync writes indexers in — see header
                  - homepage        # dashboard widget, ClusterIP — see header
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ container_item.port }}
